WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.130
Instructor: So in this video, I'm gonna demonstrate

00:02.130 --> 00:04.705
this merging workflow we've already seen

00:04.705 --> 00:06.570
and then we'll set things up

00:06.570 --> 00:09.870
so that we can then rebase and see a difference.

00:09.870 --> 00:12.210
Otherwise, if I just show you rebasing on its own,

00:12.210 --> 00:15.000
it's a little bit harder to see how it compares.

00:15.000 --> 00:17.850
All right, so I'm gonna make a new,

00:17.850 --> 00:20.160
I already made a folder called RebaseDemo.

00:20.160 --> 00:22.650
I'm gonna keep this extremely simple,

00:22.650 --> 00:26.220
in terms of what my commits are and the history I create.

00:26.220 --> 00:29.610
Currently, no git repo, so I'm going to init

00:29.610 --> 00:32.760
and I'll just work on the default master branch for now.

00:32.760 --> 00:35.370
And let's say that we're working on some projects

00:35.370 --> 00:40.370
called, I don't know, website. (chuckles)

00:40.590 --> 00:43.470
Website is gonna be a text file, just to keep things simple.

00:43.470 --> 00:45.660
All right, so I'll add that file,

00:45.660 --> 00:48.207
I will commit, just "initial commit."

00:49.200 --> 00:51.360
Just like that, nice and simple.

00:51.360 --> 00:54.180
All right, so we have our website file open.

00:54.180 --> 00:56.610
I'm not gonna be typing code, I'm just gonna pretend.

00:56.610 --> 01:00.810
We'll do something like, NAV BAR ADDED. (chuckles)

01:00.810 --> 01:01.650
That's the equivalent

01:01.650 --> 01:04.440
of somebody actually adding a navbar in, all right?

01:04.440 --> 01:09.440
So now I'm going to commit that, just -am "navbar".

01:09.600 --> 01:11.940
How about, "add navbar".

01:11.940 --> 01:15.030
Okay, so now I'm gonna switch over to a feature branch.

01:15.030 --> 01:17.970
We'll imagine that I'm working on this feature branch,

01:17.970 --> 01:18.930
whatever the feature is,

01:18.930 --> 01:22.710
I'm just gonna call it feat, just like that. F-E-A-T.

01:22.710 --> 01:24.067
So I'm on this branch,

01:24.067 --> 01:26.400
I'm gonna be working in a separate file,

01:26.400 --> 01:28.410
just to minimize conflicts.

01:28.410 --> 01:29.850
I'll also talk about what happens

01:29.850 --> 01:31.830
if we do have conflicts when we rebase.

01:31.830 --> 01:34.110
Anyway, what should my file be?

01:34.110 --> 01:37.950
Let's just call it feature.txt.

01:37.950 --> 01:40.290
All right, so I'm gonna make this incredibly basic.

01:40.290 --> 01:43.320
As I said, I'm gonna add feature, just to start,

01:43.320 --> 01:48.320
git commit -m, you know, and "begin feature".

01:50.310 --> 01:53.850
Begin feature, okay.

01:53.850 --> 01:57.259
And then, I'll do one other change in the feature.

01:57.259 --> 02:01.260
I think my changes in here are just gonna be numbers, right?

02:01.260 --> 02:06.247
So ONE, and then I'll go back, git commit -am, you know,

02:06.247 --> 02:08.037
"work on feature."

02:09.240 --> 02:12.060
Okay, so I'm now gonna switch back to master,

02:12.060 --> 02:13.323
git switch master.

02:14.400 --> 02:16.830
There's no feature file and no feature branch, right?

02:16.830 --> 02:18.240
We're on master.

02:18.240 --> 02:21.030
Let's say that somebody adds the footer.

02:21.030 --> 02:24.480
So this is new work someone has done in the meantime, right?

02:24.480 --> 02:27.240
So I'm working on my feature branch, I have some commits,

02:27.240 --> 02:29.580
then changes are merged into master

02:29.580 --> 02:30.960
that I don't have on feature.

02:30.960 --> 02:32.520
So that's what I'm doing right now.

02:32.520 --> 02:33.950
The footer was added.

02:33.950 --> 02:38.950
So I will do a git commit -am "add footer."

02:42.480 --> 02:44.220
Okay, so here's what things

02:44.220 --> 02:46.500
look like in GitKraken, currently.

02:46.500 --> 02:48.390
So we have our master branch

02:48.390 --> 02:50.160
and then we have the feature branch.

02:50.160 --> 02:52.020
If I wanna keep working on my feature branch,

02:52.020 --> 02:54.060
but I want the latest work from master,

02:54.060 --> 02:55.530
we'll pretend it's coming from, you know,

02:55.530 --> 02:58.080
somebody else did this work, not just me.

02:58.080 --> 02:59.730
I want that on my feature branch.

02:59.730 --> 03:02.070
I could do a merge on feature.

03:02.070 --> 03:04.560
So merge master into feature.

03:04.560 --> 03:06.510
And that's what I'm gonna begin with.

03:06.510 --> 03:08.460
Exactly what I did here,

03:08.460 --> 03:10.680
where we end up with a merge commit on feature.

03:10.680 --> 03:12.120
So I'm gonna do that.

03:12.120 --> 03:14.910
So I'm on master right now, right?

03:14.910 --> 03:17.103
I'm just gonna take a look at my log.

03:17.970 --> 03:20.100
We have three commits on master,

03:20.100 --> 03:22.923
but if I switch back to feat,

03:25.140 --> 03:27.480
I want to merge in the changes from master.

03:27.480 --> 03:31.680
So git merge master, I'm on the feature branch.

03:31.680 --> 03:34.530
So again, see what it looks like right now, right?

03:34.530 --> 03:36.300
I've diverged, there's new work on master.

03:36.300 --> 03:38.190
I want that on my feature branch.

03:38.190 --> 03:39.570
I'm not done with the feature,

03:39.570 --> 03:41.520
but I want the latest work from master.

03:42.750 --> 03:45.120
So it prompts me in my editor here,

03:45.120 --> 03:46.920
or if you don't have your editor configured,

03:46.920 --> 03:48.390
it'll prompt you here in your terminal

03:48.390 --> 03:50.240
with whatever your default editor is.

03:51.300 --> 03:54.990
I'm going to just close this, it's just a merge commit.

03:54.990 --> 03:57.483
So now, we have this merge commit, right?

03:58.410 --> 04:01.530
Right there, merge branch master into feat.

04:01.530 --> 04:02.970
You can see what it looks like here,

04:02.970 --> 04:05.430
we have this merge commit right there.

04:05.430 --> 04:07.620
Okay, that's not too bad I guess.

04:07.620 --> 04:09.450
So I keep working on my feature.

04:09.450 --> 04:14.040
Let's say I get to, TWO, whatever TWO is.

04:14.040 --> 04:16.050
That is more work on my feature.

04:16.050 --> 04:18.600
So I'm trying to just keep this super simple.

04:18.600 --> 04:21.597
All right, "more work on feature."

04:23.910 --> 04:25.440
Okay and then in the meantime,

04:25.440 --> 04:27.840
let's say that took me, like, half a day.

04:27.840 --> 04:30.570
Go back to master and we'll pretend we're other people,

04:30.570 --> 04:33.360
on master, a coworker finished some feature

04:33.360 --> 04:38.360
they're working on, LOGIN FORM ADDED, right?

04:39.030 --> 04:41.880
So I will add that, just to pretend I'm one of them,

04:41.880 --> 04:46.227
or I'll just do a commit -am "add login form."

04:51.259 --> 04:53.430
And the feature I'm working on,

04:53.430 --> 04:55.020
actually it would be really useful

04:55.020 --> 04:58.560
if I had that login form on that feature branch.

04:58.560 --> 05:01.230
So once again, I could do the same thing, right?

05:01.230 --> 05:03.660
I could go onto the feature branch

05:03.660 --> 05:06.993
and merge master into feature.

05:07.860 --> 05:09.540
So I'm gonna do that.

05:09.540 --> 05:11.820
'cause now there's a commit that I don't have.

05:11.820 --> 05:14.223
So I go back to feature, git switch.

05:16.410 --> 05:18.540
And then, I'm gonna do a, just git status,

05:18.540 --> 05:20.340
make sure I don't have any uncommitted stuff,

05:20.340 --> 05:22.530
git merge master.

05:22.530 --> 05:24.750
So here's what it looks like now.

05:24.750 --> 05:27.960
Here's my commit on feature that does not exist on master.

05:27.960 --> 05:31.500
Here's the commit on master, the new work, the login form.

05:31.500 --> 05:34.050
I want to merge that in to my feature branch.

05:34.050 --> 05:36.630
So I'm still not touching, I'm not changing master.

05:36.630 --> 05:39.450
Well, I am for this demo, but my feature branch

05:39.450 --> 05:42.600
is not merged into master at any point.

05:42.600 --> 05:44.730
I'm still just working on feature.

05:44.730 --> 05:47.850
So once again, it generates a new merge commit.

05:47.850 --> 05:50.370
And now, my history here, right?

05:50.370 --> 05:52.800
It doesn't look super bad, 'cause it's small.

05:52.800 --> 05:54.660
There's not a lot of merge commits,

05:54.660 --> 05:57.060
but there's another merge commit on my feature branch.

05:57.060 --> 06:00.647
And if I look at my history, git log, right?

06:02.310 --> 06:05.340
I have all this work, but then I have merge branch

06:05.340 --> 06:07.590
and then some more work, some more work.

06:07.590 --> 06:09.660
And then merge branch again.

06:09.660 --> 06:13.380
So the history of my one feature branch that I'm working on

06:13.380 --> 06:16.290
is already cluttered and I just have two merge commits,

06:16.290 --> 06:19.110
but as I do more work on this one feature

06:19.110 --> 06:22.020
and as the master branch continues to change

06:22.020 --> 06:24.540
and there are more and more commits added onto it,

06:24.540 --> 06:26.310
I end up with a lot of merge commits

06:26.310 --> 06:27.720
if I want to stay up to date.

06:27.720 --> 06:28.800
If I want my feature branch

06:28.800 --> 06:31.380
to have the latest work from master.

06:31.380 --> 06:33.570
So we end up with this sort of scenario,

06:33.570 --> 06:36.840
where my feature branch constantly has merge commits,

06:36.840 --> 06:39.420
the history is cluttered, it's muddied,

06:39.420 --> 06:41.700
it's uglier, it's harder to follow.

06:41.700 --> 06:43.260
Now, this isn't a huge deal, right?

06:43.260 --> 06:44.970
It's not the end of the world.

06:44.970 --> 06:47.910
But on a really large project with lots and lots of commits,

06:47.910 --> 06:51.330
it can be way nicer if you have a clean commit history,

06:51.330 --> 06:52.980
or a clean project history,

06:52.980 --> 06:55.940
where the commits are grouped together in a logical way

06:55.940 --> 06:59.220
and you minimize the number of those merge commits.

06:59.220 --> 07:01.500
So that is what rebasing helps us do.

07:01.500 --> 07:02.520
In the next video,

07:02.520 --> 07:05.370
I'll actually use the commands that we talked about briefly

07:05.370 --> 07:08.370
to show you how to rebase so that we don't end up

07:08.370 --> 07:10.620
with these merge commits, but instead we end up

07:10.620 --> 07:14.040
with this nice, linear history by rewriting it.

07:14.040 --> 07:15.040
So that's coming up.
